				<p>
					The takeaway matches what the cost function predicts: the SGM term is most
					useful when both P1 and P2 are nonzero (panel 3) — P1 alone or P2 alone leaves
					the half it doesn't penalise free to drift, and pushing both magnitudes too high
					(panel 4) starts erasing real depth structure. The values exposed at runtime
					over the HPS REPL (<code>P1</code>, <code>P2</code>) let us pick the operating
					point per scene without recompiling.
				</p>
				<p>
					Holding the SGM penalties fixed (the panel-3 "both penalties" configuration above),
					we then walked toward the camera rig to see how silhouette quality scales with
					range. The five frames below are ordered from <strong>farthest</strong> on the left
					to <strong>nearest</strong> on the right. Disparity is inversely proportional to
					depth, so closer objects produce a larger pixel offset between the left and right
